{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1390 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "6ab6a0ad43f8f8d784016f6cb518dd497467e9750816c42d9b4956d3f6c1cf48" :edb/db-before-t 1389 :edb/db-after-t 1390 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534702 50 #inst "2026-10-08T04:35:34.849Z" 13194139534702 true] [17592186157297 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304359 13194139534702 true] [17592186157301 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304356 13194139534702 true] [17592186157301 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304366 13194139534702 true] [17592186157302 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304373 13194139534702 true] [17592186157302 1020 #edb/ref 17592186304380 13194139534702 true] [17592186157918 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534702 true] [17592186157918 1100 "The function $y = f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation $y^{′} = y^{2} + x^{2}$. Given that the point $(1, 1)$ lies on the graph of the function, what is the value of $y^{″} \\mid _{(1, 1)}$?" 13194139534702 false] [17592186157918 1100 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}+{x}^{2}.$ Given that the point $(1,1)$ lies on the graph of the function, what is the value of ${{y}^{″}|}_{(1,1)}?$" 13194139534702 true] [17592186157918 1145 "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{′})^{′} & =({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =({y}^{2})^{′}+({x}^{2})^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =2y{y}^{′}+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the chain rule to compute $({y}^{2})^{′}.$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for ${y}^{′}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″} & =2y{y}^{′}+2x \\\\ {y}^{″} & =2y({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})+2x \\\\ {y}^{″} & =2{y}^{3}+2y{x}^{2}+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{″}|}_{(1,1)} & ={(2{y}^{3}+2y{x}^{2}+2x)|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =2(1)^{3}+2(1)(1)^{2}+2(1) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534702 true] [17592186157919 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534702 true] [17592186157919 1100 "The function $y = f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = 2x^{3} - y$.\n\nGiven that the point $(1, 2)$ lies on the graph of $y = f(x)$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $(1, 2)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n2. $(1, 2)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n3. $(1, 2)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" 13194139534702 false] [17592186157919 1100 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2{x}^{3}-y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(1,2)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(1,2)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(1,2)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n- $(1,2)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" 13194139534702 true] [17592186157919 1145 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(1,2),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)} & ={(2{x}^{3}-y)|}_{(1,2)} \\\\  & =2(1)^{3}-(2) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-(2{x}^{3}-y) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}+y \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,2)} & ={(6{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}+y)|}_{(1,2)} \\\\  & =6(1)^{2}-2(1)^{3}+(2) \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)}=0,\n$$\n\n we obtain that $(1,2)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is a critical point at $(1,2).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,2)}>0,\n$$\n\n we have a local minimum (not a local maximum) at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304356 1017 #edb/ref 17592186304357 13194139534702 true] [17592186304356 1089 "q-352578" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304356 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534702 true] [17592186304356 1100 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2y+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $P(0,0)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at $P.$\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{(0,0)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304356 1113 #uuid "63a15817-dd44-5386-b1ea-47b3a136a33c" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304356 1145 "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2y+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =2(2y+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4y+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{(0,0)} & =(4y+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,{|}_{(0,0)} \\\\  & =4(0)+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304357 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304358 13194139534702 true] [17592186304357 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534702 true] [17592186304357 1041 "field-1" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304357 1104 #edb/ref 17592186304358 13194139534702 true] [17592186304357 1110 #uuid "5d85ad5c-367a-58ce-866d-969c26e45363" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304358 1034 "8" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304358 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534702 true] [17592186304358 1111 #uuid "3b0ebf9f-b37f-5904-acae-80c7d7d2ba58" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304359 1017 #edb/ref 17592186304360 13194139534702 true] [17592186304359 1089 "q-89872" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304359 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534702 true] [17592186304359 1100 "Consider the solution curves of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-{x}^{3}={y}^{2}-2x.$ Which of the following points are stationary points of their respective solution curve?\n\n- $(-1,-1)$\n- $(0,0)$\n- $(1,1)$" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304359 1113 #uuid "48a333df-65f2-5d7d-8030-7c0c9b8d850c" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304359 1145 "Recall that a stationary point of $y(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $y$ such that ${y}^{′}(c)=0.$ \n\nFirst, we make ${y}^{′}$ the subject of the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{x}^{3}={y}^{2}-2x\\,⟹\\,{y}^{′}={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of our points into the differential equation and calculate the value of the derivative: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{y}^{′}|}_{(-1,-1)} & ={({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2x)|}_{(-1,-1)} \\\\  & =(-1)^{3}+(-1)^{2}-2(-1) \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(0,0)} & ={({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2x)|}_{(0,0)} \\\\  & =(0)^{3}+(0)^{2}-2(0) \\\\  & =0\\,✓ \\\\ {{y}^{′}|}_{(1,1)} & ={({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2x)|}_{(1,1)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+(1)^{2}-2(1) \\\\  & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304360 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304361 13194139534702 true] [17592186304360 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304362 13194139534702 true] [17592186304360 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304363 13194139534702 true] [17592186304360 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304364 13194139534702 true] [17592186304360 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304365 13194139534702 true] [17592186304360 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534702 true] [17592186304360 1041 "selection" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304360 1104 #edb/ref 17592186304361 13194139534702 true] [17592186304360 1110 #uuid "8643d4b8-5cd2-53f4-b852-10ae6e6938e3" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304361 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304361 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534702 true] [17592186304361 1111 #uuid "ec0bb55c-2478-5ca6-9028-dffe1f54ee0b" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304362 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304362 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534702 true] [17592186304362 1111 #uuid "5d28b00f-c81d-5afa-ba9c-f2ad62354a66" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304363 1034 "I only" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304363 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534702 true] [17592186304363 1111 #uuid "64f21bfd-b485-5a05-b79a-1e1631fc9c7d" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304364 1034 "III only" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304364 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534702 true] [17592186304364 1111 #uuid "7d6465f0-6fc0-501d-8a6d-7b2c2314438a" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304365 1034 "II only" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304365 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534702 true] [17592186304365 1111 #uuid "92607e7f-42a5-5e12-b3be-a47ebe07d54f" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304366 1017 #edb/ref 17592186304367 13194139534702 true] [17592186304366 1089 "q-89878" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304366 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534702 true] [17592186304366 1100 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the point $P(\\pi ,1)$ lies on the graph of the function, evaluate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at $P.$" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304366 1113 #uuid "6677f8cd-6394-55a9-96b4-fd5ec63416b0" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304366 1145 "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3y)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ into the above and simplify the resulting expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =3(3y+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =9y+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute the coordinates of our point into the above expression for the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi ,1)} & ={(9y+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi ,1)} \\\\  & =9(1)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =9-2 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304367 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304368 13194139534702 true] [17592186304367 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304369 13194139534702 true] [17592186304367 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304370 13194139534702 true] [17592186304367 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304371 13194139534702 true] [17592186304367 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304372 13194139534702 true] [17592186304367 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534702 true] [17592186304367 1041 "selection" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304367 1104 #edb/ref 17592186304370 13194139534702 true] [17592186304367 1110 #uuid "eb94a3fc-0359-5de2-852e-8b9aa0a90970" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304368 1034 "9" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304368 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534702 true] [17592186304368 1111 #uuid "d9f437e8-d7a0-55e0-9c33-0e0135821329" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304369 1034 "3" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304369 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534702 true] [17592186304369 1111 #uuid "e131f356-600e-52cb-9a00-3d89e690dfd3" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304370 1034 "7" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304370 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534702 true] [17592186304370 1111 #uuid "c9cf89ea-0d2e-5d6f-9a5d-68d65c26bedf" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304371 1034 "2" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304371 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534702 true] [17592186304371 1111 #uuid "8bf64a37-296e-54a0-8bc4-c5718135cea7" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304372 1034 "4" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304372 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534702 true] [17592186304372 1111 #uuid "aac9ad47-ff53-574a-a0c4-fcacc4efe232" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304373 1017 #edb/ref 17592186304374 13194139534702 true] [17592186304373 1089 "q-89888" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304373 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534702 true] [17592186304373 1100 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is a local maximum point of the function $f$\n- $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304373 1113 #uuid "90858788-5d30-592b-98c9-91d40707ab53" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304373 1145 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} & ={(y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} \\\\  & =0-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =(y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =y+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} & ={(y+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)|}_{(\\pi /2,0)} \\\\  & =0+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0+4-0 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is a critical point at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(\\pi /2,0)}>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe have a local minimum (not a local maximum) at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304375 13194139534702 true] [17592186304374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304376 13194139534702 true] [17592186304374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304377 13194139534702 true] [17592186304374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304378 13194139534702 true] [17592186304374 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304379 13194139534702 true] [17592186304374 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534702 true] [17592186304374 1041 "selection" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304374 1104 #edb/ref 17592186304376 13194139534702 true] [17592186304374 1110 #uuid "dcd0b64a-188b-59f2-9461-9323707d8c95" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304375 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304375 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534702 true] [17592186304375 1111 #uuid "fed94e2f-457a-5498-ae8c-02cd095a9d59" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304376 1034 "I only" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304376 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534702 true] [17592186304376 1111 #uuid "1cc0d378-80f9-53aa-87f8-d845fe6e635b" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304377 1034 "III only" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304377 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534702 true] [17592186304377 1111 #uuid "c419eea4-21e6-5e7c-99d9-584faaa2732e" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304378 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304378 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534702 true] [17592186304378 1111 #uuid "4da0ecbd-4b78-5f21-8f94-f38388c38228" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304379 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304379 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534702 true] [17592186304379 1111 #uuid "49fe9932-fa59-52ef-b9c8-e6d80f9c8a47" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304380 1017 #edb/ref 17592186304381 13194139534702 true] [17592186304380 1089 "q-89895" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304380 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534702 true] [17592186304380 1100 "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(0,-3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(0,-3)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- $(0,-3)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- $(0,-3)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304380 1113 #uuid "82946eb4-17c9-5fbc-8e27-cee185e1c664" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304380 1145 "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(0,-3),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,-3)} & ={(\\frac{x}{y})|}_{(0,-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{(-3)} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{x}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)\\cdot y-x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{y-x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-x\\cdot \\frac{x}{y})}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,-3)} & ={(\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}})|}_{(0,-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)^{2}-{0}^{2}}{(-3)^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,-3)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(0,-3)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is a critical point at $(0,-3).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,-3)}<0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local maximum (not a local minimum) at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(0,-3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304381 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304382 13194139534702 true] [17592186304381 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304383 13194139534702 true] [17592186304381 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304384 13194139534702 true] [17592186304381 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304385 13194139534702 true] [17592186304381 1000 #edb/ref 17592186304386 13194139534702 true] [17592186304381 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534702 true] [17592186304381 1041 "selection" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304381 1104 #edb/ref 17592186304383 13194139534702 true] [17592186304381 1110 #uuid "fa345378-ee66-5841-ae5f-2180cbbcc805" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304382 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304382 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534702 true] [17592186304382 1111 #uuid "d8282b27-f75c-57c6-b224-e3cb5b8d1113" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304383 1034 "I only" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304383 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534702 true] [17592186304383 1111 #uuid "c0a5a41b-39eb-5ee6-9142-6d360ef7a339" 13194139534702 true] [17592186304384